UK BANKING RISK MONITOR

UK Banking Adverse News Weekly | 09 November =96 15 November 2026

Material adverse news on the 147 UK-incorporated banks on the PRA list and =
159 UK companies

Draft =B7 2026-W46 =B7 23 item(s): 3 critical, 8 high, 12 medium

Executive Summary

FirstRand's R17.7bn provision over UK motor commission is the week's most c=
onsequential bank item, and Financial Mail reports that executives escaped =
lightly on pay despite the cost. Thames Water's debt extension and creditor=
-funded customer support show a utility rescue still in negotiation, while =
the rest is a broad restructuring and cost-cutting theme across banks and i=
nsurers. Reports of Barclays' further investment bank cuts and HSBC's Singa=
pore and Australia moves remain unconfirmed.

Adverse News by Theme
1. Regulatory enforcement
Thames Water discharged untreated sewage that travelled nearly 100 miles fr=
om Oxfordshire
HIGH
Energy & utilities
10 November | Thames Water Utilities Ltd | Follow-up of an affair first rep=
orted on 27 October

The Oxford Mail reports that Thames Water discharged untreated sewage that =
travelled nearly 100 miles from Oxfordshire earlier this week, according to=
 a map of the company's discharges on SewageMap.co.uk. The discharge is an =
environmental incident involving the company's UK operations.

Source (10 November): Oxford Mail =97 https://www.oxfordmail.co.uk/news/267=
17295.thames-water-untreated-sewage-oxfordshire-travels-100-miles/

2. Legal & conduct
HSBC's Islamic Global Equity Index Fund will exclude arms companies
MEDIUM
Bank
11 November | HSBC Bank Plc, HSBC UK Bank Plc | =A3100.0m

HSBC's Islamic Global Equity Index Fund will divest from companies earning =
more than 5 percent of revenue from weaponry, after Middle East Eye reveale=
d the fund purchased more than $100m worth of shares in companies linked to=
 the Israeli military in the first half of 2026. The fund is expected to di=
vest from RTX, Rolls-Royce, Honeywell International, BAE Systems and Palant=
ir.

Source (11 November): Middle East Eye =97 https://www.middleeasteye.net/new=
s/hsbc-sharia-compliant-fund-exclude-arms-companies-after-spending-100m-isr=
aeli-military-linked

3. Cyber & operational resilience
NATS chief apologised to aviation leaders for two September system failures
CRITICAL
Transport & infrastructure
12 November | NATS En Route | =A333.0m | Follow-up of an affair first repor=
ted on 05 November

The head of the UK's national air traffic service NATS apologised to aviati=
on leaders for two system failures in September that caused the cancellatio=
n of more than 2,000 flights. Aerospace and Mechanical Insider reports that=
 a software failure in NATS air traffic control generated an estimated =A33=
3m in domestic economic losses while NATS incurred less than =A31m.

Source (12 November): Travel Weekly =97 https://travelweekly.co.uk/news/nat=
s-chief-apologises-to-airline-bosses-for-september-system-failures

Lloyds Banking Group resolved an outage that disrupted its app and internet=
 banking for thousands of customers
HIGH
Bank
09 November | Lloyds Bank PLC

Lloyds Banking Group acknowledged that thousands of customers reported prob=
lems with its app and online banking. The group then confirmed it had resol=
ved the earlier issues and that things were back to normal. The disruption =
hit customer access to the group's digital channels before the bank restore=
d service.

Source (09 November): The Bolton News =97 https://www.theboltonnews.co.uk/n=
ews/national/uk-today/26710617.lloyds-thousands-users-report-issues-app/

NatWest: A Microsoft 365 outage disrupted NatWest's communications
MEDIUM
Bank
13 November | National Westminster Bank Public Limited Company

A Microsoft 365 outage that began on Thursday evening left companies strugg=
ling to access Outlook, Teams and other services, and affected financial se=
rvices firms including NatWest as businesses across the City grappled with =
communication failures. Microsoft attributed the problems to servers becomi=
ng unexpectedly unavailable and said there was no evidence of a cyberattack=
.

Source (13 November): cityam.com =97 https://www.cityam.com/asda-and-natwes=
t-hit-as-microsoft-outage-sparks-bad-day-for-uk-plc/

4. Capital & liquidity
FirstRand booked a R17.7bn provision over the UK motor commission
CRITICAL
Bank
09 November | FirstRand Bank Ltd | Follow-up of an affair first reported on=
 29 October

FirstRand booked a R17.7bn provision because of the UK motor commission, wh=
ich relates to the group's UK motor finance operations. Financial Mail repo=
rts that executives escaped lightly on remuneration despite the cost. The c=
harge ties the group's UK motor finance arm to the commission affair and ca=
rries a cost large enough to draw scrutiny of executive pay.

Source (09 November): Financial Mail =97 https://fm.co.za/opinion/2026-11-0=
9-a-r17-7bn-hole-and-nobody-to-blame/

Thames Water proposed pushing back =A312bn of debt to March 2028
CRITICAL
Energy & utilities
09 November | Thames Water Utilities Ltd | =A312.0bn

Thames Water Utilities proposed extending repayment of around =A312bn of de=
bts due in March 2027 until March 2028, giving it breathing space while it =
negotiates a rescue deal with Ofwat and creditors, and creditors backed the=
 move. The company also launched an initial =A3600m tranche of an enlarged =
super senior facility to extend liquidity into autumn 2027.

Source (09 November): Financial Times =97 https://www.ft.com/content/5b382a=
54-67c9-4b25-a89b-fc0ce7189015?syn-25a6b1a6=3D1

QinetiQ reported an =A386.8m half-year pretax loss after a =A3175m US impai=
rment
HIGH
Industrials
12 November | QinetiQ Group plc | =A386.8m

QinetiQ Group plc reported a first-half loss before tax of =A386.8 million,=
 reversing a =A357.3 million profit a year earlier, after a =A3175 million =
non-cash impairment charge relating to its US business. Basic loss per shar=
e was 21.6 pence. Underlying profit before tax rose 12% to =A3114.4 million=
 and the company confirmed full-year guidance.

Source (12 November): Sharecast =97 https://www.sharecast.com/news/news-and=
-announcements/qinetiq-underlying-earnings-jump-on-strong-orders-sells-us-b=
usiness--24138672.html

OSB Group's deposit interest costs hit =A3478m in the first half of 2026
MEDIUM
Bank
10 November | OneSavings Bank Plc | =A3478.0m

OSB Group paid =A3478m in interest on its =A325bn retail savings book in th=
e first half of 2026, cutting its forecast net interest margin to around 2.=
2 per cent and leaving its shares down 20 per cent year-to-date. The deposi=
t costs squeeze the margin the lender earns on its savings-funded mortgage =
book.

Source (10 November): cityam.com =97 https://www.cityam.com/why-the-city-ca=
nt-stop-talking-about-osb/

5. Strategy & restructuring
easyJet shareholders approved Apollo's =A35.7bn takeover
HIGH
Transport & infrastructure
09 November | easyJet Plc | =A35.7bn

easyJet shareholders approved US private equity firm Apollo Global Manageme=
nt's =A35.7bn takeover, with 97.07% voting in favour at a meeting on 2026-1=
1-11. The deal, agreed at 715p a share, will end easyJet's more than 26 yea=
rs on the London stock market and place the Luton-based carrier under US ow=
nership, with completion expected by the end of March next year.

Source (09 November): BBC =97 https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmvg2rw13=
kqpo

Unilever: The CMA told Unilever and McCormick to address competition concer=
ns or face an in-depth probe
HIGH
Consumer & retail
11 November | Unilever Group

The Competition and Markets Authority told Unilever and McCormick they must=
 address competition concerns in dry ingredient mixes and dry sauces sold t=
o UK retailers or face an in-depth investigation of their planned $65 billi=
on food deal. The CMA said the deal could substantially weaken competition =
in the UK grocery market, and set a November 18 deadline for remedies.

Source (11 November): RT=C9 Ireland =97 https://www.rte.ie/news/business/20=
26/1111/1598778-unilever-mccormick-food-deal-may-weaken-competition/

Metro Bank launched a formal sale process and invited takeover expressions =
of interest
HIGH
Bank
12 November | Metro Bank PLC | =A3689.0m

Metro Bank announced on Thursday that it had launched a formal sale process=
 as part of a wider strategic review aimed at accelerating growth and maxim=
ising returns for shareholders, appointing Morgan Stanley to oversee the pr=
ocess and inviting potential buyers to submit expressions of interest for t=
he entire company.

Source (12 November): cityam.com =97 https://www.cityam.com/metro-bank-puts=
-itself-up-for-sale-after-turnaround/

QinetiQ agreed to sell its US business to Serco for $292m
HIGH
Industrials
12 November | QinetiQ Group plc | =A3218.0m

QinetiQ Group plc agreed to sell its US business, QinetiQ Inc., to Serco In=
c., a subsidiary of Serco Group plc, for total cash consideration of $292 m=
illion (=A3218m). QinetiQ said it will return the majority of the proceeds =
to shareholders and reiterated full-year guidance. The sale follows a strat=
egic review of the US business announced in May.

Source (12 November): Sharecast =97 https://www.sharecast.com/news/news-and=
-announcements/qinetiq-underlying-earnings-jump-on-strong-orders-sells-us-b=
usiness--24138672.html

Thames Water creditors plan a =A375m fund for vulnerable customers
HIGH
Energy & utilities
14 November | Thames Water Utilities Ltd | =A375.0m

Sky News reports that Thames Water's creditors intend to establish a =A375 =
million fund for vulnerable customers as part of efforts to win government =
support for a private-sector rescue of the company. Sky News reports the cr=
editors will set up a =A375m social tariff fund to win Andy Burnham's suppo=
rt for a private rescue and avert the company's collapse.

Source (14 November): London South East =97 https://www.lse.co.uk/news/pres=
s-thames-water-creditors-float-new-social-tariff-fund-sky-news-iuxbjeu38tch=
ssp.html

Aldermore: FirstRand put Aldermore up for sale and weighs splitting the gro=
up
MEDIUM
Bank
09 November | Aldermore Bank Plc

FirstRand put Aldermore on the sales block in April 2026 and received initi=
al interest from around a dozen suitors; the process has narrowed to a few =
bidders seeking parts or all of the bank, with Lloyds, Investec and a Warbu=
rg-Cerberus joint bid still in the running. RBC analysts expected the wider=
 Aldermore group to fetch =A31.1bn-=A31.6bn.

Source (09 November): cityam.com =97 https://www.cityam.com/aldermore-owner=
-mulls-splitting-group-as-investec-circles-banking-unit/

Revolut will slow hiring after automating more operations with AI
MEDIUM
Bank
10 November | Revolut Bank UK Ltd

Revolut founder Nik Storonsky said the company will reduce hiring after aut=
omating more operations across the fintech with AI. Storonsky, who founded =
Revolut in 2015, reiterated hopes for a 2028 IPO after a recent =A385 billi=
on valuation. The company is exploring a dual US-UK listing.

Source (10 November): City A.M. =97 https://www.cityam.com/revolut-to-reduc=
e-hiring-after-ai-automation-campaign/

Vesuvius: A Vesuvius shareholder urges Vesuvius to give RHI Magnesita acces=
s to its data room and management before the put-up-or-shut-up deadline for=
 its =A31.16 billion offer
MEDIUM
Industrials
10 November | Vesuvius Plc | =A31.2bn | Follow-up of an affair first report=
ed on 27 October

A Vesuvius shareholder is pressing the metallurgical engineering firm to pr=
ovide RHI Magnesita with due diligence access to its data room and manageme=
nt ahead of the put-up-or-shut-up date for a =A31.16 billion deal, Seeking =
Alpha reports.

Source (10 November): Seeking Alpha =97 https://seekingalpha.com/news/46693=
80-vesuvius-holder-pushes-for-company-to-provide-rhi-with-due-diligence

Mobico: National Express will axe two Kent-to-London coach routes and close=
 a depot, putting up to 20 jobs at risk
MEDIUM
Transport & infrastructure
10 November | Mobico Ltd

National Express will terminate the 007 service (Dover, Folkestone, Canterb=
ury and London) and the 022 route (Ramsgate, Margate, Canterbury and London=
) on 2026-12-07, citing declining passenger numbers and rising operating co=
sts including escalating fuel prices. The company will also close its Pike =
Road depot in Eythorne near Dover. An anonymous employee told KentOnline th=
at up to 20 staff will lose their jobs.

Source (10 November): Kent Online =97 https://www.kentonline.co.uk/dover/ne=
ws/national-express-to-axe-kent-to-london-routes-as-job-losses-347214/

Vodafone cut 3,000 roles across its European and global support operations
MEDIUM
Technology & services
10 November | Vodafone Group Plc

Vodafone cut 3,000 roles across its European and global support services op=
erations in the first half of the year as it slashes costs to boost growth,=
 stripping out 200 million euros (=A3165.4 million) in costs across its UK =
and European business, including 50 million euros (=A341 million) of saving=
s in the UK after its 2025 merger with Three UK to create VodafoneThree.

Source (10 November): London Evening Standard =97 https://www.standard.co.u=
k/business/business-news/vodafone-germany-european-three-uk-india-b1304257.=
html

HSBC explored giving its Singapore operations greater autonomy under Projec=
t Sirena
MEDIUM
Bank
11 November | HSBC Bank Plc

HSBC Holdings has been exploring options to make its Singapore business mor=
e independent and reduce reliance on China and Hong Kong, an initiative int=
ernally named Project Sirena. The bank plans to combine its main Singapore =
banking units first and may later move other regional operations there, cre=
ating a second Asian base alongside Hong Kong. HSBC says there are no firm =
decisions yet.

Source (11 November): The Straits Times =97 https://www.straitstimes.com/bu=
siness/hsbc-explores-bolstering-singapore-to-balance-china-reliance?ref=3Dl=
atest-headlines

Nomura Bank International: Nomura explores sale of Laser Digital crypto uni=
t after losses pushed European operations into the red
MEDIUM
Bank
11 November | Nomura Bank International Plc.

Nomura Holdings is exploring the sale of its Zurich-based crypto subsidiary=
 Laser Digital Holdings after losses at the entity pushed the brokerage's e=
ntire European operation into a quarterly deficit. Nomura has held discussi=
ons with potential buyers including Marex Group, which is evaluating a poss=
ible acquisition, and no party has reached a final decision. The Japan Time=
s reports the development.

Source (11 November): The Japan Times =97 https://www.japantimes.co.jp/busi=
ness/2026/11/12/companies/nomura-europe-crypto-unit-sale-talks/

Barclays will cut about 200 investment bank jobs starting next week
MEDIUM
Bank
13 November | Barclays Bank Plc

Barclays Plc is preparing to cut about 200 positions across its investment =
bank starting next week, mainly in New York and London, in addition to the =
roughly 5,000 jobs it said last month it was planning to trim. Bloomberg re=
ports the reductions, citing people with knowledge of the matter; a Barclay=
s spokesperson declined to comment.

Source (13 November): Bloomberg =97 https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles=
/2026-11-13/barclays-to-cut-about-200-jobs-across-its-investment-bank

HSBC struggles to sell its $20bn retail deposit book two months after launc=
hing the sale
MEDIUM
Bank
14 November | HSBC Bank Plc | =A320.0bn

HSBC gave its sell-side bankers the green light to sell its retail deposit =
book two months ago, and the Australian Financial Review reports the sale t=
ask looks more challenging than it first seemed in a precarious market. The=
 Australian Financial Review reports HSBC Australia's bankers at Citi face =
an uphill battle finding a buyer.

Source (14 November): Australian Financial Review =97 https://www.afr.com/s=
treet-talk/hsbc-s-uphill-battle-to-sell-20b-deposit-book-in-a-precarious-ma=
rket-20261115-p61e9c

